`ObservableObject` is a protocol for **reference types** (classes) that need to notify views of changes — unlike `@State`, which is for value types owned by one view. Mark it `@Published` and every mutation automatically fires `objectWillChange`, which any subscribed view uses to know it needs to re-render.

```swift
class CartViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var items: [Item] = []
    @Published var total: Double = 0
}
```

A view consumes it with `@StateObject` (if the view creates and owns the instance) or `@ObservedObject` (if it's handed one created elsewhere). Getting `@StateObject` vs `@ObservedObject` wrong — using `@ObservedObject` for an instance the view itself creates — is a classic bug: the view can recreate the object on every re-render, silently resetting state that should have persisted.

```swift
class SearchViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var query = ""
    @Published var results: [Item] = []
    private var cancellable: AnyCancellable?

    init() {
        cancellable = $query
            .debounce(for: .milliseconds(300), scheduler: RunLoop.main)
            .removeDuplicates()
            .sink { [weak self] text in
                self?.performSearch(text)
            }
    }
}
```

`$query` is the **publisher** for the `@Published` property — the dollar-prefixed projected value, same convention as `@Binding`. `debounce` collapses a burst of keystrokes into one emission 300ms after typing stops; `removeDuplicates` skips redundant searches if the text ends up unchanged. Without `[weak self]` in the `sink` closure, this view model can never deallocate — the subscription holds a strong reference to itself indefinitely.

```swift
func badSetup() {
    // Wrong: the returned AnyCancellable is discarded immediately
    $query.sink { print($0) }
}
```

A Combine subscription returns a token (`AnyCancellable`) that **owns the subscription** — drop the token and Combine cancels the subscription right away, since nothing is keeping it alive. The fix is always storing it: a `private var cancellable: AnyCancellable?` property, or `.store(in: &cancellables)` into a `Set<AnyCancellable>` for multiple subscriptions on one object.

The inverse failure is just as real: storing the cancellable in a property that outlives the object using it, without ever cancelling — the subscription (and anything it captures) stays alive as long as the storage does, which is exactly the kind of retain-cycle-adjacent leak that Instruments' memory graph catches.
